  "findings": "'reorder item' is not supported as the dominant source of delays. It is delay-relevant for items and is over-represented in slow item cases, but stronger delay evidence points to other activities, especially 'payment reminder', 'package delivered', and 'create package'. For items, 'reorder item' ranks 3rd by median sojourn time and 5th by median waiting time, and has no measurable pooling time. In contrast, 'payment reminder' has the highest item waiting time and pooling time, while 'package delivered' has the highest item sojourn time. Slow item cases show 'reorder item' and 'item out of stock' are both disproportionate, suggesting association with slow cases, but not unique dominance. Structural child-count correlations are near zero and not significant, so the number of items per order/package does not explain flow-time delays. Interaction patterns also weaken the claim: 'reorder item' occurs as a simple 1 employee + 1 item + 1 product event, whereas 'payment reminder' and 'create package' involve multi-item interactions and show stronger delay/pooling evidence. Overall conclusion: 'reorder item' is correlated with some slow cases and contributes to delay, but it is less important than other activities and interaction/pooling effects as an explanation for most delays.",
